  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 1: Does the driver actually work?" prompt="Before a channel, a receiver, or any equalizer is anywhere in the picture, confirm the transmitter alone launches a full, clean signal. Just a generator and an Eye Scope - look at what's actually coming out of the driver."
            hint="Don't stop at the eye's &quot;height&quot; percentage - it normalizes to whatever swing is actually there, even a small one. Check the swing figure right next to it."
            explanation="The transmitter's output amplitude is set to about 0.04, roughly a sixth of the usual 0.25 - the driver is alive and the Eye Scope's height reads a reassuring 100% (it's normalized to whatever swing is actually present), but the swing readout right next to it shows only about 0.16 FS where a healthy transmitter launches close to 1.0 FS. This is the single most important bring-up habit for reading any eye diagram, real or simulated: a &quot;wide open&quot; percentage only means the signal is clean relative to itself - always also check the absolute swing before concluding the driver is actually putting out a healthy signal. The fix is turning the TX output level/drive strength back up to spec."/>
</LABCHAIN>
